<TopicPage slug="subjects/real-estate/foundational/property-and-ownership">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Real Estate, and it assumes no legal background. The subject
    is built on one idea and a vocabulary of perhaps twenty words; once the vocabulary is
    yours, the licensing examination's property questions become reading comprehension. Take
    the ideas slowly — the law here is old, precise, and more logical than it first appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain the <em>bundle of rights</em> and use it to answer what is actually being bought, leased or borrowed against;</li>
    <li>separate real property from personal property, and apply the tests that decide whether an object is a fixture;</li>
    <li>distinguish the main estates — fee simple, life estate, leasehold — and the main forms of co-ownership;</li>
    <li>identify easements, liens, covenants and public limits, and say which rights each one removes;</li>
    <li>describe how deeds, recording and title insurance make ownership provable.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever land is mentioned, ask <em>which rights, held by whom, for how long?</em>
    — because the physical ground is almost never what is being traded.
  </p>

  <h2>The house that five parties own at once</h2>
  <p>
    Consider one ordinary property. The legal owner holds title in <strong>fee simple</strong>
    — the fullest form of ownership. A power company holds a 2-metre <strong>easement</strong>
    along one side of the lot: its right of passage to reach its lines. A recorded deed
    restriction forbids outbuildings. A bank holds a <strong>mortgage</strong> — its claim on
    the property as security for the loan. And a tenant occupies the basement flat on a
    one-year lease — exclusive possession for the term.
  </p>
  <p>
    Five parties hold five different bundles of rights in the same physical land, each legally
    real, each priced differently. That is the central reframe of this subject: real estate is
    not dirt; it is <em>legal rights</em> attached to dirt. Nearly every question in the field —
    what is being bought, what a lease conveys, why a neighbour can walk across your lot — is
    answered by asking which sticks of which bundle belong to whom.
  </p>

  <h2>The bundle of rights</h2>
  <p>
    English-speaking law pictures ownership as a <strong>bundle of rights</strong> — often
    drawn as a bundle of sticks. The classic sticks are the rights to <em>possess</em> (be
    there), <em>use</em> (enjoy and exploit it), <em>exclude</em> (keep others off — the
    deepest right, and the basis of trespass law), <em>transfer</em> (sell, give or will it),
    and <em>encumber</em> (pledge it as security). The metaphor earns its keep in one sentence:
    <em>each stick can be separated from the bundle and held by someone else</em>. The
    utility's easement is one stick. The mortgage is another. The tenant's lease is a bundle
    carved out for a term. What a homeowner buys, what a tenant rents and what a bank holds
    as security are different bundles tied to the same ground.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the bundle, the estates, and what title proves" widget="equationStep" config={bundleCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the panel runs from the abstract (the bundle) to the
    concrete (title as its proof). The order is deliberate: every transaction starts by
    asking which sticks are in play, then how long they last (the estate), then what has been
    removed (encumbrances), and only then how the deal is evidenced (the deed and the
    records). Keep the five panels as your mental checklist for every later topic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> ownership of land is a bundle of separable rights, not a single
    thing. The next question is the law's first sorting problem: which objects count as
    <em>land</em> at all?
  </p>

  <h2>Real property versus personal property — and the fixture problem</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Real property</strong> is land and everything permanently attached to or
    associated with it: the surface, the soil and minerals beneath (unless separately sold),
    the airspace above, the buildings, and <em>appurtenances</em> — rights that run with the
    land, such as a water right or an easement enjoyed over a neighbour's lot.
    <strong>Personal property</strong> is everything else: furniture, vehicles, shares, tools.
    (Moveable, tangible personal property is called <strong>chattel</strong>; property that
    exists only as a right — a debt, a patent, a copyright — is intangible but still owned and
    transferable.) The boundary sounds clean until objects move across it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The movers are called <strong>fixtures</strong> — items that began as personal property
    and became real by attachment. A ceiling fan on a shop shelf is a chattel; bolted to a
    living-room ceiling, it is part of the house and conveys with the sale. Is the chandelier
    part of the house, or the seller's treasured possession she takes down? Law answers with
    tests, memorably bundled as <strong>MARIA</strong>: <em>Method</em> of attachment (bolted
    in or merely plugged in?), <em>Adaptability</em> to the property's use (custom-fitted to
    this building?), <em>Relationship</em> of the parties (a tenant's installation is treated
    differently from an owner's), <em>Intention</em> of whoever installed it, and any express
    <em>Agreement</em> — which is why purchase contracts list exclusions in writing. Two
    carve-outs matter commercially: <strong>trade fixtures</strong> — a dentist's chair, a
    shop's counters — remain the business tenant's personal property even though bolted down,
    provided they are removed before the lease ends and any damage repaired; and crops grown
    by a tenant (<em>emblements</em>) stay personal property with a right to harvest — the
    growing corn is real property, the picked ear is a chattel.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that "if it is bolted down, it is part of the house". It
  seems reasonable — attachment feels decisive. But attachment is only the first MARIA test:
  intention and agreement can un-bolt in law what is bolted in fact, which is exactly why the
  seller taking a "fixed" chandelier, and the tenant taking a "fixed" dentist's chair, can
  both be legally correct. The professional reflex is to resolve fixtures in the written
  contract, not in the doorway argument at handover.</p>

  <h2>How ownership is held: estates and co-ownership</h2>
  <p>
    Ownership is legally described by its <strong>estate</strong> — the duration and quality
    of the rights held. The freehold family: <strong>fee simple absolute</strong> is the full
    bundle, of indefinite duration, freely inheritable — what "owning a home" means to most
    people. <strong>Fee simple defeasible</strong> is fee simple that ends or reverts if a
    stated condition is broken ("so long as the land is used as a school" — the grantor keeps
    a <em>reversionary interest</em>, and such conditions still occasionally bite). The
    <strong>life estate</strong> lasts for a named person's lifetime, after which the property
    passes to the <em>remainderman</em> — "the house to my wife for life, then to my children"
    creates exactly this, and the related <strong>reverse mortgage</strong> lets an older
    owner trade home equity for income while retaining possession for life. The leasehold
    family is carved out of the fee: estates for years (a fixed term), periodic tenancies
    (month to month), tenancies at will, and tenancies at sufferance (the holdover tenant) —
    all conveying possession for time while the fee stays with the landlord. Valuation
    follows the estate, not the dirt: a 99-year leasehold flat and a freehold flat on
    identical land are different assets with different prices.
  </p>
  <p>
    Several people can hold the same estate, and the legal form decides what happens when one
    dies. <strong>Tenancy in common</strong>: co-owners hold possibly unequal shares, each
    with the right to possess the whole; a deceased co-owner's share passes to <em>her
    heirs</em>, and any co-owner may sell her share without permission.
    <strong>Joint tenancy</strong>: created with the four unities (time, title, interest,
    possession) and the distinctive <strong>right of survivorship</strong> — a deceased joint
    tenant's share vanishes into the survivors automatically, outside the will and probate.
    The married-couple variant is <strong>tenancy by the entirety</strong> in some states, and
    community-property states have their own spousal regime. The difference is a worked
    example in itself: two siblings buy a cottage as joint tenants; one dies — the survivor
    owns the whole cottage, full stop. As tenants in common, the deceased sibling's half
    instead goes to her heirs, who may be strangers to the survivor and co-owners of a lawn
    mower they cannot divide. Beyond co-ownership stand ownership <strong>entities</strong> —
    partnerships, LLCs and corporations (which shield personal assets and fractionalise
    ownership), trusts (which separate control from benefit) — and the two high-rise forms:
    the <strong>condominium</strong> (fee simple ownership of a unit plus shared ownership of
    common elements) versus the <strong>co-operative</strong> (a corporation owns the
    building; residents own shares and a proprietary lease) — forms that decide who you sue,
    how you finance, and what happens on default.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that co-owners' shares always pass to their heirs — that
  "each of us owns our half" means the same thing in every deed. With tenants in common it
  does; with joint tenants it does not, because survivorship extinguishes the deceased share
  before inheritance law ever applies. Choosing the form is a decision with probate, tax and
  family consequences, made once, in the deed — and many buyers never notice which form they
  signed.</p>

  <h2>The sticks others hold: easements, encumbrances and title</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>easement</strong> is the right to use another's land for a purpose — the least
    disruptive encumbrance and the most common. <strong>Appurtenant</strong> easements benefit
    a neighbouring parcel (a right of way to a landlocked lot — there is a dominant estate
    that enjoys the benefit and a servient estate that bears it) and run with the land;
    <strong>easements in gross</strong> benefit a person or company instead (the utility
    company's power-line corridor). Easements are created expressly in writing, by implication
    (an obvious pre-existing use when land is divided), by necessity (the landlocked parcel's
    access), or by <strong>prescription</strong> — open, notorious, hostile and continuous use
    for the statutory period (often 10–20 years) ripens into a right. The same elements make
    <strong>adverse possession</strong>, the doctrine by which long, open, unchallenged
    occupation can ripen into ownership itself — the law's answer to land left idle while
    someone else puts it to work. A <strong>licence</strong> (mere revocable permission — your
    cinema ticket) is the easement's weaker cousin and must not be confused with it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beyond easements, the encumbrance family: <strong>liens</strong> — creditors' claims
    against the property (mortgages, unpaid taxes, mechanics' liens), ranked by priority,
    which is why the first mortgagee is repaid first in foreclosure; <strong>private
    restrictions</strong> — covenants and association rules binding successive owners (fence
    heights, paint palettes); and <strong>public limits</strong> — zoning, building codes, and
    <strong>eminent domain</strong>, the state's power to take property for public use with
    just compensation. Water adds its own layer, and it splits the United States in two:
    eastern states follow the <strong>riparian</strong> doctrine (land next to water carries a
    shared right to reasonable use), while most western states follow <strong>prior
    appropriation</strong> — "first in time is first in right", a use-it-or-lose-it regime
    where the right need not belong to the adjacent landowner at all. Every one of these
    removes a stick from the owner's bundle, and the market reprices each: an encumbered title
    is a different asset.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which is why <strong>title</strong> — the proof of the bundle — closes every transaction.
    Real property may be acquired by purchase, by inheritance, by gift (which needs intent,
    delivery and acceptance) or, in the rare case, by adverse possession; but whatever the
    route, ownership passes by <strong>deed</strong>, and deeds come in grades matching the
    promise. A <strong>general warranty deed</strong> warrants the whole chain of title; a
    <strong>special warranty deed</strong> warrants only the grantor's own period of
    ownership; a <strong>quitclaim deed</strong> warrants nothing at all — it transfers
    whatever the grantor happens to have, which might be nothing, and is the instrument of
    choice for clearing clouds and settling disputes. Recording the deed in the county where
    the land sits puts the world on notice of who owns what, and priority rules generally
    protect the <strong>bona fide purchaser</strong> — one who buys in good faith with no
    knowledge of competing claims. <strong>Title examination</strong> traces the chain of
    recorded deeds, easements and liens so the buyer knows exactly which bundle is changing
    hands, and <strong>title insurance</strong> underwrites the residue of doubt.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that adverse possession rewards a stealthy squatter who
  hides on land unnoticed. The doctrine requires the opposite: the use must be <em>open,
  notorious and hostile</em> — a fence built in plain sight, a driveway driven every day, a
  neighbour farming a corner for years while the owner says nothing. What actually ripens into
  a right is the <em>owner's long silence in the face of visible use</em>; the statute of
  limitations exists precisely so that disputes are brought while evidence is fresh.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A seller removes the dining-room chandelier before closing, calling it "family heirloom". The buyer objects. Name the test framework and the factor most likely to decide it.</li>
    <li>Two friends buy a house as joint tenants. One dies insolvent, owing money. What happens to her half?</li>
    <li>What is the difference between an easement appurtenant and an easement in gross?</li>
    <li>Why does a general warranty deed fetch a better price than a quitclaim deed for the same land?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) MARIA. Intention (and any express agreement in the
    contract) is usually decisive: a heirloom clearly intended to be taken, or an exclusion
    written into the contract, survives attachment — though the safest answer is that the
    contract should have said so. (2) Her half passes to the surviving joint tenant by right
    of survivorship, outside her will and outside her estate — which is why creditors cannot
    reach it after death, and why lenders often require both borrowers to sign.
    (3) Appurtenant benefits a neighbouring parcel and runs with that land (dominant and
    servient estates); in gross benefits a person or company personally, such as a utility
    corridor. (4) The warranty deed carries the seller's promise against defects in the whole
    chain of title, so the buyer takes less risk; the quitclaim promises nothing — it conveys
    only whatever the grantor has, which may be nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Real estate trades legal rights, not dirt: the bundle of rights (possess, use, exclude, transfer, encumber), each stick separable.</li>
    <li>Real property is land and what is attached; personal property is everything else. Fixtures cross the line — decided by MARIA, with trade fixtures and emblements as carve-outs.</li>
    <li>Estates measure duration: fee simple (forever), fee simple defeasible (until a condition breaks), life estate (for a life), leasehold (for a term).</li>
    <li>Co-ownership forms matter at death: tenancy in common passes shares to heirs; joint tenancy passes to survivors by right of survivorship.</li>
    <li>Easements, liens, covenants and public limits each remove sticks; water rights split between riparian and prior-appropriation regimes.</li>
    <li>Deeds grade the promise (general warranty, special warranty, quitclaim); recording gives notice; title examination and insurance manage the residue of risk.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Every later topic in this programme stands on this lesson. Valuation (next unit) prices
    bundles — and must first know which rights are being valued. Finance turns the
    <em>encumber</em> stick into mortgages, notes and foreclosure. Contracts and closing run
    the deed–recording–title machinery you have just met at transaction speed. When those
    lessons ask "what exactly is being bought?", the answer is the question this page equipped
    you to answer: which bundle, held by whom, with which sticks missing.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two worked miniatures show the doctrine priced in practice. The first is <strong>lien
    priority in foreclosure</strong>: a house worth 400,000 dollars carries a 300,000-dollar
    first mortgage, a 50,000-dollar second mortgage, and a 10,000-dollar mechanics' lien
    recorded last but for work done first. Foreclosure distributes by priority, not by size:
    the first mortgagee is paid in full, the second takes what remains of the 100,000-dollar
    surplus, and the mechanics' lien holder — depending on the jurisdiction's special priority
    rules — may find itself paid from dregs or nothing. This is why lenders require title
    searches, and why subcontractors fight for statutory lien priority. The second is
    <strong>easement creation by prescription</strong>: a neighbour has driven across the
    corner of a lot to reach a garage for nineteen years, openly, without permission, while
    the owners said nothing; in a jurisdiction with a twenty-year period, one more year of the
    same use ripens a revocable permission into a permanent property right — and the owner who
    finally objects discovers that two decades of silence was the gift. Both miniatures teach
    the same lesson: interests are created by records, by statutes and by <em>conduct</em>,
    and the professional's job is knowing which clock is running.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three deeper layers show where the foundation leads. <strong>Severed rights</strong>: the
    bundle's sticks can be split vertically as well as temporally — mineral estates sold
    beneath a retained surface, transferable development rights, billboard and solar air-rights
    leases — creating "split estates" whose valuation and disputes are a specialty of their
    own. <strong>Recording systems</strong>: the Anglo-American deed-recording regime makes
    title a matter of traced documents and priority rules — first to record wins, subject to
    exceptions — while the Torrens registration system (Australia and scattered
    jurisdictions) makes title conclusive by state guarantee; the choice of system shapes
    everything from conveyancing cost to fraud risk. <strong>Regulatory takings</strong>: the
    live constitutional question of how far public regulation — zoning, environmental limits,
    rent control — may shrink the bundle before it becomes a compensable "taking", a line
    courts still draw case by case, and the reason the bundle-of-rights metaphor remains a
    working legal instrument rather than a classroom antique.
  </p>

